  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A disturbance will bring the potential for \nheavy rain and flash flooding across the Piedmont on Friday \nafternoon and evening.\n\nA backdoor front will stall south of the region along the VA/NC\nborder tonight. This frontal boundary will provide a focal point\nfor storm activity to develop off of during the afternoon and\nevening hours on Friday. Across the upper levels, the axis of a\n500mb trough looks to swing just west of the region across\nwestern Tennessee by Friday afternoon. As this happens, it will\nprovide additional forcing for ascent across the region. With\nnear 2.0 inch PWATs remaining in place across the Piedmonts of\nVA/NC Friday afternoon, there is a decent chance for flash\nflooding primarily east of the 29 corridor in Virginia and North\nCarolina. These PWATs combined with the stalled front and upper\nlevel forcing will lead to storms capable of producing 3-5 inch\nper hour rainfall rates at times, which if these storms \ntrain over the same areas they will likely lead to flash\nflooding concerns. The largest flash flooding concerns look to\nbe during the later evening hours from 6pm to midnight for the\nareas aforementioned above. Some uncertainty remains in the\ntransient nature of storms tomorrow, as faster moving storms\nalong the stalled boundary may lead to less chances for flash\nflooding. Also, some CAM guidance keeps this boundary south of\nthe area, which would also keep the heaviest rainfall out of\nthe area leading to a much lower flash flood threat.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...After continued showers and thunderstorms on \nSaturday, drier weather returns Sunday and continues into early \nnext week.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms look to continue across the area on\nSaturday during the early morning hours across the Piedmont as\nthat backdoor front lifts north out of the region. During the\nafternoon hours, a cold front looks to approach the region from\nthe northwest, which will lead to some isolated shower and\nthunderstorm chances on Saturday afternoon and evening. By\nSunday, surface high pressure pushes into the region behind the\naforementioned cold front leading to much drier air over the\nregion through the middle of next week. \n\nThe aforementioned surface high pressure system looks to\neventually push east off the Mid-Atlantic states coastlines \ninto the western Atlantic leading to the return of warm moist \nair and daily scattered shower and thunderstorm chances.\n\nTemperatures overall look to remain near normal for this time of\nyear, with highs in the mid to upper 80s and low 90s across the\nPiedmont, and upper 70s to low 80s west of the Blue Ridge.",
